TI  - Three-year follow-up results of the prospective European Multicenter Cohort Study on Cyanoacrylate Embolization for treatment of refluxing great saphenous veins
AU  - Thomas M. Proebstle
AU  - Jens Alm
AU  - Sameh Dimitri
AU  - Lars Rasmussen
AU  - Mark S Whiteley
AU  - James Lawson
AU  - Alun H. Davies
JO  - Journal of Vascular Surgery Venous and Lymphatic Disorders
PY  - 2020
VL  - 9
IS  - 2
SP  - 329
EP  - 334
DO  - 10.1016/j.jvsv.2020.05.019
AN  - 32599306
AB  - OBJECTIVE\nCyanoacrylate closure (CAC) of refluxing saphenous veins has demonstrated excellent safety and effectiveness results in feasibility and pivotal studies. This article provides the 36-month follow-up results of the prospective multi-center non-randomized cohort study.\n\n\nMETHODS\nA total of 70 patients were enrolled in a prospective multicenter study conducted at 7 centers in 4 European countries and received treatment of a solitary refluxing great saphenous vein with endovenous cyanoacrylate embolization without the use of tumescent anesthesia or post-procedure compression stockings. The primary effectiveness endpoint was freedom from recanalization (closure rate) of the great saphenous vein at 6 months. Safety was assessed by occurrence of adverse events after the procedure and during the 6-month follow-up period. Quality of life (QoL) and clinical improvement parameters were measured before and after the procedure and through a 12-month follow-up period. Anatomic success and clinical improvement were assessed through 36-months post-procedure.\n\n\nRESULTS\nOut of 70 treated patients, 64 (91%) patients were available for 3-year follow-up. The closure rates by Kaplan-Meier life table methods at 6-, 12-, 24-, and 36-month time points were 91.4%, 90.0%, 88.5%, and 88.5%, respectively. Through 36 months, the improvement in change of the mean VCSS score over time was statistically significant by dropping from 4.3 at baseline to 0.9 at the 36-month follow-up (p<0.001).\n\n\nCONCLUSIONS\nThe 3-year follow-up results of the prospective, multicenter eSCOPE study demonstrated the continued anatomical and clincal effectiveness of cyanoacrylate embolization over an extended follow-up period.
